diagnosis_text:
A thorough four-phase investigation of the live Kath4 topology found no fault. The user's report that 172.16.30.x machines are unreachable from 10.5.0.1 (pc1, Campus 2) is not supported by any current evidence.

Direct reachability probe: pc1 → pc12 (172.16.30.12) completed with 0% packet loss. This alone rules out any routing or forwarding gap on the forward and return path, since a successful ICMP round-trip requires both directions to work.

Routing plane: all nine OSPF routers report 18 Full-state routes. The prefix 172.16.30.0/24 is directly connected on mswitch0's vlan30 SVI (172.16.30.254/24) and is propagated unchanged through router16 → router7 → router17 → sshswitch, appearing as a FIB-selected OSPF entry (O>*) on every transit device. No prefix is missing, suppressed, or pointing to a black hole.

ACL and traffic control: infra_sweep found zero nftables or iptables drop rules on any device; tc_snapshot found no tbf or netem qdiscs on any interface. Neither an ICMP-pass/TCP-drop ACL nor bandwidth throttling is present.

Intermittent/transient history: dhcp_link_history found no carrier-change events; pressure_sweep found no flap scripts or stress-tool processes; OSPF adjacency ages show continuous stability since lab startup roughly seven minutes before the investigation began. There is no mechanism or historical trace consistent with a past fault on this path.

Root cause: no anomaly exists in the current lab state. The reported inability to reach 172.16.30.x from 10.5.0.1 is unconfirmed and leaves no evidence of a causal mechanism.

Fix: no corrective action is required. If the user observes the symptom again, a contemporaneous packet capture (tcpdump on switch3 or mswitch0 vlan30) would be needed to catch a transient event that the current steady-state snapshot cannot see.
